Subject: The layout export carries the two-prototype cache kind and the IsHTMLDDA bit

What this does
  - `JSAotGenLayout` gains `reg_pic_proto2` (the cache entry kind of a holder two prototypes up),
    `off_object_htmldda_byte` and `object_htmldda_mask` (where `JSObject.is_HTMLDDA` lives), found the
    same way the `holey` bit is.

Why we need it
  A compiled body reads a method inherited through two prototypes (a class that extends a class) from
  the function's own cache entry, and tests an object for null without a call into the engine; both
  need these constants from the engine's own layout so a change to either breaks the unit's hash.

--- a/quickjs-aot-rt.h
+++ b/quickjs-aot-rt.h
@@ -138,6 +138,9 @@
        array, as the engine's own fast store does, since filling a hole has
        bookkeeping the inline path does not do. */
     uint32_t off_object_holey_byte, object_holey_mask;
+    /* The cache entry kind of a holder two prototypes up, and the byte and mask of
+       `is_HTMLDDA`: an inline null test of an object has to ask it. */
+    uint32_t reg_pic_proto2, off_object_htmldda_byte, object_htmldda_mask;
     /* The rest of the light-frame header a generated native entry writes, so
        it can build the frame the engine's own light call builds. The header is
        the frame's first `light_prefix_slots` words; `off_regrun_fr` is where
--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -61306,6 +61306,23 @@
             }
         }
     }
+    out->reg_pic_proto2 = REG_PIC_PROTO2;
+    {
+        /* `is_HTMLDDA` likewise. */
+        JSObject o;
+        const uint8_t *q = (const uint8_t *)&o;
+        size_t i;
+
+        memset(&o, 0, sizeof(o));
+        o.is_HTMLDDA = 1;
+        for (i = 0; i < sizeof(o); i++) {
+            if (q[i] != 0) {
+                out->off_object_htmldda_byte = (uint32_t)i;
+                out->object_htmldda_mask = q[i];
+                break;
+            }
+        }
+    }
     /* The light-frame header a generated native entry writes itself. It builds
        the same frame `regvm_light_fill` builds, field for field, so the
        offsets are the frame's own and the flag values are the engine's. */
